7. Firmware architecture
Capture data path

Plan for two independent paths:

Digital capture: Configure PC0–PC5 as inputs without pull resistors unless a particular test requires them. Use a timer for sample timing. First implement an interrupt-driven prototype, then validate whether a timer/DMA arrangement can read GPIOC->IDR on this MCU as intended. Do not assume a DMA request path exists without checking RM0390.

CAN capture: Configure PB8/PB9 as CAN1 RX/TX, alternate function AF9, with the CAN controller in silent mode. The external RS pin is already tied high to prevent physical transmission.

For the PC application, I recommend a simple protocol containing a capture identifier, sample rate, trigger configuration, sample buffer and overflow flags. Keep captured CAN frames and sampled GPIO traces in separate timestamped streams.
Capture performance
Use 1 MS/s as the first benchmark, then test 2 MS/s and 5 MS/s. These are targets, not promised hardware capabilities.

A 64 KiB buffer holding one byte per sample would record:
Sample rate	Capture duration
1 MS/s	65.5 ms
2 MS/s	32.8 ms
5 MS/s	13.1 ms

You cannot stream 5 MB/s continuously over USB Full Speed; capture-then-transfer is the straightforward approach for the first version.
8. Important decisions before PCB manufacturing
There are four unresolved design-validation items:
1. Digital protection: Select the exact six-channel ESD solution and verify its capacitance, leakage and clamping voltage. A real 5 V-tolerant or 24 V-tolerant analyzer requires a different front end.
2. USB connector and crystal: Confirm the exact purchasing part numbers, footprint pad numbers and oscillator loading.
3. CAN TVS and bus environment: Select protection for the intended CAN bus voltage/transient conditions, and decide whether galvanic isolation is required.
4. Firmware timing: Validate the STM32 timer, GPIO sampling and DMA approach on the STM32F446 before finalizing any capture-rate specification.
These do not prevent you from starting the schematic tonight, but they should be resolved before sending the board for fabrication.
